speeches.en:
Thank you, Honourable Speaker. Bill to amend Law No. 2/99 (Tourism Act of the Maldives). The purpose of submitting the bill on behalf of the government is to introduce into Law No. 2/99 (Tourism Act of the Maldives) a system for leasing sites to a state-owned company for developing tourist resorts and for leasing sites for developing tourism training resorts; to set out in law the rules for leasing sites for such purposes and operating them; to include in the law the rules on extending the period given to develop sites leased for resort development, and the rules on determining the lagoon area of islands leased for tourism purposes; to issue licences for providing services of planning and operating tourism-related trips and set out in law the basic principles of providing services under that licence; and in addition to bring the other amendments needed to strengthen enforcement of the law. To achieve the aforementioned purposes, the following matters are included in this bill. Introducing into the Tourism Act of the Maldives a system for leasing sites to a state company for developing a tourist resort and leasing sites for developing tourism training [resorts]. Setting out in the law the rules for operating leased sites, and including in the law the rules on extending the period given to develop sites leased for developing such resorts and the rules on determining the lagoon area of islands leased for tourism purposes. Issuing licences for providing services of planning and operating tourism-related trips and setting out in law the basic principles of providing services under that licence, and in addition bringing the other amendments needed to strengthen enforcement of the law. Given that tourism is the main pillar of the Maldivian economy, and that strengthening the legal framework needed to run and develop this sector is always the government's top priority, I note that the inclusion in this amendment of measures to further strengthen the tourism sector and pave the way for Maldivian tourism to be sustainable is a reason this amendment is important. An important aspect of this bill is that a system of leasing sites to state companies for developing tourist resorts

is being introduced in law. In addition, it is proposed to set out in this law the rules for leasing sites to develop tourism training resorts. Through these two things, as well as expanding tourism with state participation, the way will be paved to build the training and skilled workforce the sector needs. We note that clearly setting out in law the rules for leasing and operating such sites is very important to ensure transparency and fairness. Another important aspect included in the law being amended is the rules on extending the period given to develop sites leased for resort development. In this way, the challenges faced when development work remains unfinished for a long time will be resolved, and investors will get an additional opportunity. Also included in the law is the determination of the lagoon area of islands leased for tourism purposes, and the rules for it. I also note this as an important step being taken to resolve all challenges in using islands' land and lagoons and to determine how the areas can be used in the most desirable way. The law being amended today came into force on 16 May 1999. So far 15 amendments to the said law have been submitted and passed. And I inform all you dear Honourable Members that what is being submitted today is the 16th amendment. Looking at how this bill is drafted, this bill is a set of amendments to the Tourism Act of the Maldives. They are important steps being taken to give the legal force needed to develop the tourism sector sustainably and bring its benefits to the Maldivian economy. They are amendments designed so that Maldivian tourism becomes more competitive in the world tourism market and the whole country benefits from it. Accordingly, the amendments being proposed are: to amend Article 2 of the said law; to amend subsections (a) and (c) of Article 4 of the said law and add a subsection after (c); to add a number after number 3 of subsection (a) of Article 5 of the said law as follows; to add a subsection after subsection (j) of Article 5 of the said law; to add 3 subsections after subsection (h) of Article 6 of the said law; to add two articles after Article 6 of the said law; to amend subsections (c) and (g) of Article 9 of the said law; to change the headings after Articles 16 and 20 of the said law and add 5 articles; to amend Article 28 of the said law; to amend Article 32 of the said law;

to amend Article 33 of the said law; to add 2 subsections after subsection (b) of Article 34 of the said law; to add a subsection after subsection (c) of Article 45-1 of the said law; to amend Article 48 of the said law; to amend subsection (h) of Article 53 of the said law; and, in connection with the amendments this law brings to the said law, for the amendments that need to be made to the regulations drawn up under that law and the regulations that need to be newly drawn up to be drawn up within 90 days from the date this law comes into force and published in the Government Gazette of the Maldives, I submit this bill to this honourable Majlis. Thank you, Honourable Speaker.
